title: No Worship in a Foreign Land
author: julie-brashear
excerpt: >-
  As we look ahead a few chapters, we can see that God does offer redemption and
  healing
date: 2026-07-20
category: HOSEA
---
A new organization launched when I was in high school.&nbsp; Two mothers, Candace Lightner and Cindi Lamb, founded Mothers Against Drunk Driving (MADD). Candace had lost her daughter to a repeat drunk driver. Cindi’s daughter had been severely injured in a separate crash. Out of their grief, these two women built a movement that changed how my generation and those that followed thought about drinking and driving.

They worked for legislation to strengthen consequences for impaired driving, judicial enforcement of laws, and public education. The education focused on helping others understand the real consequences of driving while impaired.&nbsp;

What made MADD’s warnings so effective wasn’t just that they were serious. They were specific. We didn’t just hear “drinking and driving is dangerous.” We heard exactly what could happen: You could hurt or kill someone else, or lose your own life. You could lose your driver’s license. You could go to jail. You could damage your chances of getting into college or landing a job. We remembered the consequences because they were communicated so clearly. Vague warnings are easy to shrug off. Specific ones are much harder to ignore.

A similar pattern shows up thousands of years earlier, in the words of the prophet Hosea. God’s warnings spelled out to Israel in chapter 9&nbsp; weren’t vague either; they were relevant, specific, and impossible to misunderstand. Hosea warns them of loss of homeland (v. 3), loss of joy and agricultural provision (vv. 1–2), loss of worship (vv. 4–6), and hostility toward spiritual leadership (vv. 7–9).&nbsp;

**Loss of homeland (v. 3)**

The most fundamental consequence was losing the land itself. This wasn’t just any territory.&nbsp; It was the land God had promised to Abraham back in Genesis 12, the foundation of Israel’s identity as a chosen people. Losing it meant losing the very thing that made them who they were. The land represented God’s presence. Instead of a flourishing life in the promised land, they'll be taken captive by Assyria, just like their ancestors were once trapped in Egypt. Deuteronomy 28:63–65 had already warned that exile would be the ultimate curse for covenant unfaithfulness, so this consequence wasn’t a surprise.&nbsp;

**Loss of joy and agricultural provision (vv. 1–2)**

This section of the prophecy warns them to rejoice not. Their happiness stemmed from unfaithfulness and pagan living, not a genuine relationship with Yahweh. They habitually celebrated their fruitful harvest and relied on the land’s abundant provision for their security. Psalm 119:1–2 reminds us that real blessing and joy come from walking in God’s ways and seeking him wholeheartedly; exactly what Israel had abandoned. The threshing floor and wine vat, which nourished them daily, would no longer sustain them. This too echoes Deuteronomy 28, which describes failed crops as one of the direct consequences of turning away from God. As the food supply fails, their joy which depended on it will diminish.

**Loss of worship (vv. 4–6)**

Israel would also lose the ability to worship obediently. Their offerings would no longer be acceptable; their food would carry the weight of mourning instead of blessing, and they wouldn’t even be able to celebrate their sacred festivals. In Amos 5:21–23, God rejects religious rituals that aren’t matched by real faithfulness. Worship without genuine devotion means nothing to God, and here, Israel loses even the empty rituals they’d been leaning on. Nettles or thorns will overtake their precious things and tents. This symbolizes the desolation and decay of their impending captivity.&nbsp;

**Hostility toward spiritual leadership (vv. 7–9)**

Finally, in their iniquity, they will not listen. The prophets, who were supposed to watch over the people, are instead called foolish and mad. This may describe the people’s corruption and refusal to listen to true prophets. One can argue that some of the “prophets” themselves acted corruptly. The evil was so deep that Hosea compares this corruption to the shameful events at Gibeah (Judges 19), a low point in Israel’s history.

**Finding Hope**

These are strong warnings, and this passage itself does not offer much hope to the people of Israel. As we look ahead a few chapters, we can see that God does offer redemption and healing. He says he will love them freely and turn his anger away from them (Hosea 14:4). God’s forever love will cover their multitude of sins and betrayals.&nbsp;

**Home**

We have an even greater hope: redemption through Jesus Christ. While Israel faced exile for its unfaithfulness, Christ promised his followers a permanent home (John 14:2-3). United with Christ, we are part of the kingdom of God, which has no end. Not only do we look forward to the future glory of being present with Christ, we already are a part of his forever kingdom. Because he has made his home with us, nothing can separate us from his love (Romans 8:38–39). Jesus stated that the kingdom of God is in the midst of us (Luke 17:21).&nbsp;

**Access**

Where Israel lost the ability to offer acceptable worship, Christ became the perfect sacrifice once for all. Believers now have direct access to God, not through empty ritual, but through a relationship made possible by grace (Hebrews 10:19–22). When we fully rely on him for our salvation, Christ frees us from the striving of trying to make ourselves acceptable.&nbsp;

**Leadership**

Israel suffered and was called corrupt because they ignored the words of the prophets.Christ is our perfect prophet, priest, and king. As we submit to his rule and cling closely to his words, we flourish in a way that contrasts with the corruption of Israel in Hosea’s day. Abiding in Christ by yielding to him, results in us bearing fruit (John 15:4-5).&nbsp;

**Joy**

Perhaps most strikingly, because Israel disconnected from God, it lost its joy, whereas believers experience a joy that doesn’t depend on circumstances at all. Our faith in Jesus Christ results in our being filled with an inexpressible and glorious joy (I Peter 1:8). This supernatural joy comes from the Holy Spirit within us. (Galatians 5:22)

The very specific warnings in Hosea 9 remind us how seriously God takes unfaithfulness; but they also set the stage for how completely his grace covers it. What Israel lost through disobedience, believers now receive as a gift through Christ: a lasting home, restored worship, sacrificial leadership, and unshakable joy.This is not because we’ve earned it, but because grace has already paid the price.
